### 2. Recursive Approach (For conceptual understanding, but slow for large $n$)

This approach directly translates the mathematical definition $F(n) = F(n-1) + F(n-2)$. Be aware that this has an exponential time complexity $O(2^n)$ because it recalculates the same values many times.

```typescript
/**
 * Calculates the n-th Fibonacci number recursively.
 * WARNING: This method is very inefficient for large values of n.
 *
 * @param n The index of the Fibonacci number to calculate (must be a non-negative integer).
 * @returns The n-th Fibonacci number.
 * @throws {Error} If n is negative.
 */
function fibonacciRecursive(n: number): number {
    if (n < 0) {
        throw new Error("Input must be a non-negative integer.");
    }
    if (n === 0) {
        return 0;
    }
    if (n === 1) {
        return 1;
    }
    return fibonacciRecursive(n - 1) + fibonacciRecursive(n - 2);
}

// Example Usage:
console.log(`Fibonacci(6) (Recursive): ${fibonacciRecursive(6)}`); // Output: 8
```

