Here is a TypeScript function that computes the median of an array of numbers.

The median is the middle value of a dataset when it is sorted. If the dataset has an odd number of elements, the median is the middle element. If it has an even number of elements, the median is the average of the two middle elements.

```typescript
/**
 * Computes the median of an array of numbers.
 *
 * @param numbers An array of numbers.
 * @returns The median of the array, or null if the array is empty.
 */
function findMedian(numbers: number[]): number | null {
    if (numbers.length === 0) {
        return null; // Return null for an empty array
    }

    // 1. Sort the array numerically
    const sortedNumbers: number[] = [...numbers].sort((a, b) => a - b);
    const length: number = sortedNumbers.length;

    // 2. Check if the length is odd or even
    if (length % 2 === 1) {
        // Odd length: The median is the middle element
        const middleIndex: number = Math.floor(length / 2);
        return sortedNumbers[middleIndex];
    } else {
        // Even length: The median is the average of the two middle elements
        const upperMiddleIndex: number = length / 2;
        const lowerMiddleIndex: number = upperMiddleIndex - 1;

        const middle1: number = sortedNumbers[lowerMiddleIndex];
        const middle2: number = sortedNumbers[upperMiddleIndex];

        return (middle1 + middle2) / 2;
    }
}

// --- Examples ---

// Example 1: Odd number of elements
const oddArray: number[] = [1, 3, 2, 5, 4];
// Sorted: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]. Median is 3.
const medianOdd = findMedian(oddArray);
console.log(`Median of [${oddArray}]: ${medianOdd}`); // Output: 3

// Example 2: Even number of elements
const evenArray: number[] = [10, 4, 8, 2];
// Sorted: [2, 4, 8, 10]. Median is (4 + 8) / 2 = 6.
const medianEven = findMedian(evenArray);
console.log(`Median of [${evenArray}]: ${medianEven}`); // Output: 6

// Example 3: Array with duplicates and negative numbers
const mixedArray: number[] = [-5, 10, 0, 10, 3];
// Sorted: [-5, 0, 3, 10, 10]. Median is 3.
const medianMixed = findMedian(mixedArray);
console.log(`Median of [${mixedArray}]: ${medianMixed}`); // Output: 3

// Example 4: Empty array
const emptyArray: number[] = [];
const medianEmpty = findMedian(emptyArray);
console.log(`Median of []: ${medianEmpty}`); // Output: null
```

Here is a TypeScript class for a stack implementation. I've used a standard JavaScript array internally to store the stack elements.

```typescript
/**
 * A simple implementation of a Stack data structure.
 * It supports push (add to top), pop (remove from top), and peek (view top) operations.
 */
class Stack<T> {
    // The internal array to store the stack elements.
    private items: T[];

    /**
     * Initializes a new instance of the Stack class.
     */
    constructor(): void {
        this.items = [];
    }

    /**
     * Adds an element to the top of the stack.
     * @param element The element to be added to the stack.
     */
    public push(element: T): void {
        this.items.push(element);
        console.log(`Pushed: ${element}`);
    }

    /**
     * Removes and returns the element at the top of the stack.
     * Returns undefined if the stack is empty.
     * @returns The element that was removed, or undefined if the stack is empty.
     */
    public pop(): T | undefined {
        if (this.isEmpty()) {
            console.warn("Cannot pop from an empty stack.");
            return undefined;
        }
        const poppedItem = this.items.pop();
        console.log(`Popped: ${poppedItem}`);
        return poppedItem;
    }

    /**
     * Returns the element at the top of the stack without removing it.
     * Returns undefined if the stack is empty.
     * @returns The element at the top, or undefined if the stack is empty.
     */
    public peek(): T | undefined {
        if (this.isEmpty()) {
            console.warn("Cannot peek at an empty stack.");
            return undefined;
        }
        // The top element is the last element in the array
        return this.items[this.items.length - 1];
    }

    /**
     * Checks if the stack is empty.
     * @returns True if the stack contains no elements, false otherwise.
     */
    public isEmpty(): boolean {
        return this.items.length === 0;
    }

    /**
     * Returns the current number of elements in the stack.
     * @returns The size of the stack.
     */
    public size(): number {
        return this.items.length;
    }
}

// --- Example Usage ---
console.log("--- Creating a Stack of Numbers ---");
const numberStack = new Stack<number>();

numberStack.push(10);
numberStack.push(20);
numberStack.push(30);

console.log(`Current size: ${numberStack.size()}`);
console.log(`Top element (peek): ${numberStack.peek()}`); // Should be 30

const poppedValue = numberStack.pop();
console.log(`Popped value: ${poppedValue}`); // Should be 30

console.log(`New top element (peek): ${numberStack.peek()}`); // Should be 20

numberStack.pop();
numberStack.pop();

console.log(`Is the stack empty? ${numberStack.isEmpty()}`); // Should be true

// Trying to pop from an empty stack
numberStack.pop();
```

Here is a TypeScript function that converts a snake\_case string to camelCase.

This implementation handles common cases, such as converting strings like `first_name` to `firstName`.

```typescript
/**
 * Converts a snake_case string to camelCase.
 *
 * @param snakeCaseString The input string in snake_case format (e.g., "user_first_name").
 * @returns The converted string in camelCase format (e.g., "userFirstName").
 */
function snakeToCamel(snakeCaseString: string): string {
  if (!snakeCaseString) {
    return "";
  }

  // 1. Split the string by the underscore character.
  // 2. Map over the resulting array.
  // 3. For every part, capitalize the first letter and lowercase the rest.
  // 4. Join the parts together.
  const camelCaseString = snakeCaseString
    .split('_')
    .map((word: string, index: number) => {
      if (word.length === 0) {
        return "";
      }
      if (index === 0) {
        // The first word remains entirely lowercase (or as is, depending on strict requirements,
        // but for standard camelCase conversion, we keep the first part as is).
        return word;
      }
      // Capitalize the first letter and append the rest of the word in lowercase.
      return word.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + word.slice(1).toLowerCase();
    })
    .join('');

  return camelCaseString;
}

// --- Examples ---

